id: rule-array-assign-aliasing
title: Forgetting aliasing
topic: Arrays
example-of: ARRAY-ASSIGN
derived: false

task:
  Use the rule ARRAY-ASSIGN to decide whether this triple can be derived.
  Its precondition does not say whether $j$ is different from $i$.

given:
  Position written: $i$
  Value written: $1$

pre: a[j] = 0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a)
post: a[j] = 0

program:
  {P}
  {R1}
  a[i] := 1      // w
  {Q}

verdict: invalid

step: The substitution of the array
rule: ARRAY-ASSIGN
focus: w
text:
  The rule replaces the array $a$ in $Q$ by the updated array
  $a[i |-> 1]$, which is $a$ with the value 1 at position $i$.
calc:
  (a[j] = 0)[a <- a[i |-> 1]]     // Q is a[j] = 0
  = a[i |-> 1][j] = 0             // a replaced by a[i |-> 1]
set: R1 = a[i |-> 1][j] = 0

step: The two cases of the read
rule: ARRAY-ASSIGN
focus: R1
text:
  We do not know whether $j$ is equal to $i$. At position $i$ the updated
  array holds 1; at any other position $j$ it holds $a[j]$.
calc:
  a[i |-> 1][j] = 0
  = (j = i && 1 = 0) || (j != i && a[j] = 0)    // read of a[i |-> 1] at position j
  = j != i && a[j] = 0                          // 1 = 0 is false, and false || A is A
set: R1 = j != i && a[j] = 0

step: The write must be defined
rule: ARRAY-ASSIGN
focus: w
text:
  The write is at position $i$, so the rule also adds $def(a[i])$, which
  is $0 <= i < sizeof(a)$.
set: R1 = j != i && a[j] = 0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a)

step: P does not imply R1
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, R1
text:
  >>> «P» ==> «R1»
vc: a[j] = 0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a) ==> j != i && a[j] = 0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a)
status: fails
counterexample: a = [4, 0, 9], i = 1, j = 1
why:
  Here $P$ holds but $j != i$ does not. $a[i]$ and $a[j]$ are the same
  cell, so the write sets $a[j]$ to 1, and $Q$ is false at the end.
